course: history
lesson: 13
title: Rome: from a town on seven hills to a republic
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Romans told a legend that their city was founded in 753 BC by twin brothers, Romulus and Remus, who as babies were saved and fed by a she-wolf. Archaeology tells a plainer story: villages on seven hills by the river Tiber grew together into a town.
explain: Rome first had kings. In about 509 BC the Romans threw out the last, cruel king and swore never to have one again. Instead they made a republic, where citizens elected leaders for one year at a time.
explain: Imagine having two class captains who must agree on everything: neither can boss everyone around. Rome had two consuls at the top for that very reason, and a Senate of experienced men to advise them.
explain: Rome fought Hannibal of Carthage, who crossed the Alps with elephants, and won. Then the general Julius Caesar grew so powerful that senators killed him, and his adopted son Augustus became the first emperor.
fact: Legend says Rome was founded in 753 BC by the twins Romulus and Remus, who were fed by a she-wolf, but archaeology shows Rome grew from small villages on hills by the river Tiber.
fact: Rome grew on seven hills beside the river Tiber, at a place where people could cross the river, good for trade and defence.
fact: Rome was first ruled by kings; in about 509 BC the Romans threw out the last king and swore never to have a king again.
fact: In the Roman Republic citizens elected leaders for one year at a time, and two consuls shared the top job so neither could become too powerful.
fact: The Senate was a council of experienced, mostly rich men who advised the leaders and controlled money and foreign affairs; the USA and other countries still have a Senate.
fact: Rich old families were patricians and ordinary citizens were plebeians; the plebeians went on strike by leaving the city until they won more rights.
fact: The Romans wrote their laws on twelve tablets displayed in the forum, and many countries' laws today are based on Roman law.
fact: Hannibal of Carthage, in North Africa, marched an army with war elephants over the Alps into Italy and beat the Romans many times, but Rome did not give up and in the end defeated Carthage.
fact: Julius Caesar, a brilliant general, conquered Gaul, today's France, took power in Rome, and was killed by senators in 44 BC who feared he wanted to be king.
fact: The month July is named after Julius Caesar, and the words Kaiser and Tsar come from Caesar.
fact: After Caesar's death his adopted son Octavian won the civil wars and became Augustus, the first emperor, in 27 BC; he said the Republic still went on, but real power was now his, and the month August is named after him.
exercise: According to legend, who founded Rome? | Romulus and Remus | Julius Caesar and Augustus | Hannibal and Leonidas | Zeus and Hera, king and queen of the gods
exercise: What animal fed the twins in the legend? | A she-wolf | A lioness | An eagle | A cow
exercise: In which year does the legend say Rome was founded? | 753 BC | 1453 AD | 44 BC | 776 BC
exercise: What does archaeology show about the start of Rome? | It grew from villages on hills by the Tiber | It was built by the twins in one single day | It was founded by Greeks who sailed from Troy | It began as a Viking camp
exercise: Beside which river did Rome grow? | The Tiber | The Nile in Egypt | The Thames | The Danube
exercise: Why was Rome's spot good? | People could cross the river there, good for trade and defence | It was on an island far out in the sea | It had gold mines under every one of its seven hills | It was hidden in a desert
exercise: What did the Romans do in about 509 BC? | Threw out their last king | Crowned their first emperor | Built the Colosseum | Lost to Hannibal
exercise: In the Republic, how long did elected leaders serve? | One year at a time | For life | Ten years | One day
exercise: Why were there two consuls? | So neither could become too powerful | Because one of them was always ill in bed | One ruled by day and the other ruled by night | To make the army twice as big as before
exercise: What was the Senate? | A council of experienced men who advised leaders | A Roman football team that played in the Colosseum | A temple on a hill | The emperor's own bodyguard
exercise: What were ordinary Roman citizens called? | Plebeians | Patricians | Consuls | Senators
exercise: How did the plebeians win more rights? | They went on strike and left the city | They burned down the Senate house in anger | They voted for a new king | They moved to Greece
exercise: On how many tablets did the Romans first write their laws? | Twelve | Two | One hundred | Seven
exercise: How did Hannibal surprise Rome? | He crossed the Alps with war elephants | He sailed up the Tiber in a submarine | He dug a tunnel under Rome | He came by camel from China
exercise: Which land did Julius Caesar conquer? | Gaul, today's France | Egypt and India | Britain and Ireland | China
exercise: Who became the first Roman emperor? | Augustus | Julius Caesar | Romulus | Hannibal
test: my cousin says Romulus and Remus are proven history. what's the truth? | It is a legend; Rome grew from villages | It is proven by the old bronze statue of the wolf | They were real Greek kings | The wolf's bones were found
test: Julius Caesar was the first Roman emperor, right? | No, Augustus was the first emperor | Yes, he was crowned emperor in 44 BC | Yes, he ruled for 40 years | No, Romulus was
test: If one consul wanted to start a silly war and the other said no, what happened? | Neither could act alone, so it was blocked | The first consul won anyway and marched off to war | The Senate was closed | The king decided
test: Why did Romans hate the word king for centuries? | They had thrown out a cruel king | Kings were Greek | The word was rude in Latin | They had never had a king
test: You see a football badge with a wolf feeding twins. Which city's legend is it? | Rome | Athens | Sparta | Carthage
test: Why do we have a month called July? | It is named after Julius Caesar | It means "hot month" in Greek | It was named by the Vikings | It is named after a Roman river
test: Hannibal beat Rome many times. Who won in the end? | Rome defeated Carthage | Carthage conquered Rome | Nobody, it was a draw in the end | The Greeks, who beat both of them
test: Senators killed Caesar in 44 BC. What were they afraid of? | That he wanted to be king | That he would leave Rome | That he would free all slaves | That he would lose a war
test: oi why do germans say kaiser and russians said tsar? | Both words come from Caesar | Both mean "wolf" | Both come from Hannibal | They are Viking words
test: A town picks its spot where a river can be crossed. Why is that clever? | Traders and travellers pass through it | Rivers keep the town warm in the winter | No enemies can swim | Fish jump straight into the houses
test: Augustus kept saying the Republic still existed. Was he right? | No, real power was now with him as emperor | Yes, nothing changed at all when he took over | Yes, the consuls ruled him | No, he made Rome a Greek city
exam: Identify the person who in 27 BC became the first emperor of Rome. | Augustus | Julius Caesar | Romulus | Hannibal
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the Roman Republic? | Citizens elected magistrates for terms of one year, two consuls sharing the highest office | A single hereditary king governed for life, advised by his sons and the priests of Jupiter | The army appointed a dictator each decade | The Senate was elected by all women
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the foundation of Rome. | The tale of Romulus and Remus is legend; the city arose from settlements upon hills by the Tiber | The foundation by the twins is proven by documents | The city was established by Carthage | The city was founded in 1453
exam: Ascertain the reason for which Julius Caesar was slain by senators in 44 BC. | They apprehended that he intended to make himself king | He had been defeated by Hannibal in Italy | He had abolished the month of July | He had surrendered all of Gaul to the enemy
